WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:10.320
We have good news. This is good news from the&nbsp;
standpoint of being less bad. US Weekly new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:16.560
jobless claims are at their lowest level&nbsp;
since the start of. The coronavirus pandemic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:22.000
there was a bigger than expected drop in new&nbsp;
unemployment claims that we learned about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:28.080
yesterday, we are at a pandemic era low. The&nbsp;
Department of Labor released this information&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:34.400
yesterday morning. The new jobless claims&nbsp;
for the week ending March 20 came in at six&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:39.600
hundred and eighty four thousand. The expectation&nbsp;
was seven hundred and thirty thousand and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:46.080
revised expectation was seven hundred and eighty&nbsp;
one thousand. So almost one hundred thousand fewer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:51.520
initial jobless claims than expected over&nbsp;
the last week. Continuing claims are down to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:56.080
three point eight million, three point eight&nbsp;
million remaining unemployed versus the four&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.080 --> 00:01:00.880
million and four point one million that were&nbsp;
expected and revised. This is the first time new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:06.960
jobless claims have been under 700000, going back&nbsp;
to mid-March of twenty twenty, which is the start&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:14.240
of the pandemic. And really, you know, week to&nbsp;
week, the numbers can kind of go up and down what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:19.360
we want to look at as trends and new lows. And we&nbsp;
are seeing both the continuing downward trend and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:25.360
a new low. Now, this is not a while. Everything's&nbsp;
fixed. That's fantastic. It's not that at all.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:30.000
And we still have so many different issues,&nbsp;
including jobs that are coming back as part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:36.640
time that used to be full time. And what's funny&nbsp;
is the when when a Democrat is in the White House,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:41.680
the right always says they're not telling&nbsp;
you this and this and this and this.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:47.200
But I tell you this stuff all the time.&nbsp;
There's reasons that these numbers are not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:52.400
glorious numbers. And this only has some to&nbsp;
do with Joe Biden accelerating vaccination,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:57.680
certainly as a factor. But there is a seasonality&nbsp;
to this pandemic that is similar in much of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:03.760
world. You know, I have no interest in making&nbsp;
things partizan when they don't particularly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:08.000
need to be. There's no doubt that there are&nbsp;
some jobs coming back as part time rather&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:12.960
than full time. As businesses go from being open&nbsp;
a little bit to being fully open, hopefully in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:16.080
the next six months, some of those part time&nbsp;
jobs will become full time. They won't all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:22.000
all all sorts of other caveats. We've not&nbsp;
dealt with the minimum wage. There are still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:27.840
wage issues in general. But I think if we are&nbsp;
looking to kind of take a cautiously optimistic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:32.800
approach on the pandemic, yesterday we saw&nbsp;
that although cases have stopped declining,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:37.520
hospitalizations are still declining, albeit&nbsp;
more slowly. Deaths are still declining&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:42.720
and vaccinations are increasing. If we think two&nbsp;
to three months out as we get into early summer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:48.480
that's potentially a pretty good looking&nbsp;
situation economically. We are now starting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:54.480
to get new analyzes from Goldman Sachs. And I&nbsp;
know many people will say, David, Goldman Sachs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.480 --> 00:03:01.680
if you go back to the Goldman Sachs analyzes of&nbsp;
April twenty twenty in terms of vaccine rollout&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.240 --> 00:03:09.680
and economic numbers, they were the most accurate&nbsp;
I'm aware of. They correctly predicted approval&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:15.280
for a vaccine in late October, early November.&nbsp;
They correctly predicted that you would start to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:21.200
see health care workers vaccinated towards the end&nbsp;
of 2020. Trump was saying it would be way sooner.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:24.400
Some doom and gloom ers were saying&nbsp;
it wouldn't be under until the middle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:29.040
of twenty twenty one. Goldman Sachs has been&nbsp;
excellent on this with their predictions. I'm not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:33.440
telling you. Go and get Goldman financial adviser.&nbsp;
I'm not telling you anything about financial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:40.720
advisors. But the Goldman analysis is that the end&nbsp;
of twenty twenty one, particularly Q4, is going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:46.080
look very good by most of the economic indicators&nbsp;
that that we look at. So the point here is much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:51.840
work to be done, not the time to start going&nbsp;
crazy, as they've been doing in some spring break&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:57.600
hotspots. But the economic numbers are improving,&nbsp;
the vaccination numbers are improving, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:03.760
we're hoping that the blip in cases is going to&nbsp;
continue down over the next two to three months.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:11.840
I am sure that I will get some calls from viewers&nbsp;
in the next segment about what is going on with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.840 --> 00:04:17.360
vaccination and the pandemic. Let me know your&nbsp;
thoughts on the expected trajectory. You can find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.360 --> 00:04:23.920
me on Twitter at David Pakman. And you can also&nbsp;
find the show on Twitter at David Pakman show.

